N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals four eighths (2/4). Bar 1 D4 E4 F#4 A4 eighths; bar 2 A4 quarter, quarter rest; bar 3 rest; bar 4 A4 F#4 E4 E4 eighths; bar 5 D4 quarter, quarter rest; bar 6 rest; bar 7 F#4 F#4 E4 E4 eighths; bar 8 D4 quarter, quarter rest. Pitch set D-E-F#-A (no fa, so the climb is pentatonic), range D4-A4, largest interval a major third. The earlier draft's cues at bars 1-2 and 5-6 fell on sung bars; now each cue covers a sung line and the silent bar after it, where a child really plays. The analyzer reads level 2 (pentatonic, rests); its Ontario line reads Grade 5 only because it counts the two-sharp key signature and the I-V chord symbols, which SK children never read; only beat and pitch direction are claimed. Originality: a climbing and falling pentatonic line is common property; the words avoid the up-the-hill and down-again lines of "The Grand Old Duke of York", whose tune differs; checked also against "Hot Cross Buns", "Frère Jacques" and "This Old Man". "Pan in 'A' Minor" is a minor-key calypso; nothing of it is used.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether SK children can fit a four-bell pattern into a three-beat gap at quarter = 104, whether "copy or go the other way" is clear as a partner answer, which original 1987 release "Pan in 'A' Minor" first appeared on, and how a steelpan educator would want the history told. A classroom trial, a Trinidadian steelpan educator and a second score reader must settle these.
